==Interim appointment==
After Graham died, Governor [[Henry McMaster]] was tasked with appointing a successor to serve the remainder of his term. Lieutenant Governor [[Pamela Evette]] was considered a possible appointee, while U.S. Representative [[Joe Wilson]] declined consideration.<ref name= "PoliticoJuly12">{{Cite web |title= Lindsey Graham's death will kick off a breakneck campaign for his Senate seat |url= https://www.politico.com/news/2026/07/12/lindsey-graham-death-replacement-process-00994113 |last1= Hernandez |first1= Alec |date= July 12, 2026 |access-date= July 12, 2026 |website= [[Politico]] |language= en}}</ref> On July 13, President [[Donald Trump]] publicly recommended that McMaster appoint [[Darline Graham]], Lindsey's sister, to serve the remainder of the term,<ref>{{Cite web |last1= Bresnahan |first1= John |last2= Sherman |first2= Jake |last3= Mutnick |first3= Ally |last4= Desiderio |first4= Andrew |last5= Weiss |first5= Laura |date= July 13, 2026 |title= Graham's death rocks Senate as Congress returns |url= https://punchbowl.news/article/senate/graham-death/ |website= Punchbowl News}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title= Trump Endorses Lindsey Graham's Sister for Senate Seat |url= https://www.devdiscourse.com/article/politics/3949605-trump-endorses-lindsey-grahams-sister-for-senate-seat |access-date= July 13, 2026 |website= Devdiscourse |language= en}}</ref> as did [[Senate Majority Leader]] [[John Thune]].<ref>{{Cite web |title= Thune says it would 'make a lot of sense' for Lindsey Graham's sister to replace him in Senate |url= https://www.cnn.com/politics/video/thune-lindsey-grahams-sister-to-replace-him-in-senate-digvid |access-date= July 13, 2026 |website= [[CNN]] |language= en}}</ref> McMaster selected Darline on July 13<ref name="WaPoJuly13">{{Cite news |url= https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2026/07/13/trump-recommends-lindsey-grahams-sister-serve-rest-his-senate-term/ |title= Lindsey Graham Sister Picked To Serve The Rest of His Senate Term |first1= Alexei |last1= Kosseff |first2= Cat |last2= Zakrzewski |newspaper= The Washington Post |date= July 13, 2026 |access-date= July 13, 2026}}</ref> and she was sworn in on July 14.<ref>{{Cite news |url= https://thehill.com/homenews/5967990-darline-nordone-lindsey-graham-senator-oath/ |title= Darline Graham, sister of late Sen. Lindsey Graham, sworn in as senator |first1= Helen| last1= Huiskes| work= The Hill| date= July 14, 2026| access-date= July 14, 2026}}</ref> She is the [[List of the first women holders of political offices in the United States|first woman]] to represent South Carolina in the U.S. Senate.<ref>{{Cite web |last1= Popat |first1= Shrai |title= Darline Graham, sister of Lindsey Graham, sworn in to fulfill his Senate term |url= https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/jul/14/darline-graham-nordone-south-carolina-senator |access-date= July 14, 2026 |date= July 14, 2026 |website= The Guardian |language= en}}</ref> On July 20, Darline announced that she would run for her first full term in office.<ref>{{Cite web |title= Sen. Darline Graham will run for full term to replace brother Lindsey |url= https://www.politico.com/news/2026/07/20/darline-graham-launches-senate-campaign-south-carolina-lindsey-graham-01004994 |access-date= July 20, 2026 |website= Politico |language= en}}</ref>

